[{"id":"928080a8","n":1,"exam":["final"],"year":439,"years":[439,434,433],"rep":5,"appears":"Final: 439, 434, 433","stem":"A 65-year-old man presented to the urology outpatient department complaining of dysuria for 2 months associated with passage of cloudy urine with bubbling on micturition. Patient gave a history of recurrent left iliac fossa pain for 2 years. Physical examination is unremarkable. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","opts":[{"k":"A","t":"Transitional cell carcinoma of the bladder."},{"k":"B","t":"Squamous cell carcinoma of the bladder."},{"k":"C","t":"Infiltrating colon cancer."},{"k":"D","t":"Diverticular disease."}],"ans":"D","ansAdded":false,"srcNote":"(Luts,BPH,UTI) Source note (Final 433 A Q13 (p. 179)): Answer: D “Diverticular disease can give rise to fistulae to other viscera.The patient with a colovesical fistula usually complains of dysuria and the passage of cloudy urine, with bubbling on micturition (‘pneumaturia’).” Davidson","img":[],"expl":"Recurrent UTI symptoms with cloudy urine and bubbles (pneumaturia, fecaluria) plus long-standing left iliac fossa pain = colovesical fistula, most often from diverticular disease (sigmoid diverticulitis).","why":{"A":"TCC presents with painless haematuria, not pneumaturia.","B":"SCC (chronic irritation, schistosomiasis) doesn't cause bubbles in the urine.","C":"Colon cancer can cause a fistula, but the 2-year history of LIF pain points to diverticulitis.","D":"Correct."}},{"id":"62484026","n":2,"exam":["final"],"year":439,"years":[439,437,436],"rep":3,"appears":"Final: 439, 437, 436","stem":"A 55-year-old male presented with lower urinary tract symptoms. His work revealed normal urine analysis and culture, normal PSA and low urine flow. An Ultrasound of the abdomen and pelvis showed enlarged prostate (30cc) with elevated post void residual. What is the appropriate management for this patient?","opts":[{"k":"A","t":"Behavioral modification"},{"k":"B","t":"Alpha blocker"},{"k":"C","t":"5 alpha reductase"},{"k":"D","t":"Transurethral resection of prostate"}],"ans":"B","ansAdded":false,"srcNote":"(LUTS, BPH, UTI) Source note (Final 439 F Q15): The related recall specifies a prostate size of 34 on ultrasound. A. Tamsulosin A (LUTS, UTI, BPH)","img":[],"expl":"Bothersome LUTS with low flow and raised PVR in a man with a modest (30 cc) prostate, normal PSA and urine → start an α1-blocker (fast relief of the dynamic component). 5ARIs are for larger glands.","why":{"A":"Behavioural changes alone are for mild symptoms without objective obstruction.","B":"Correct.","C":"5ARIs work best in larger glands (&gt; 30–40 cc) and act slowly.","D":"Surgery needs an indication (retention, stones, renal impairment, failed medication)."}},{"id":"27025412","n":3,"exam":["final"],"year":439,"years":[439,429],"rep":2,"appears":"Final: 439, 429","stem":"A 65 year old man presented with moderate storage and voiding lower urinary tract symptoms. Past medical and surgical history is unremarkable except for mild hypertension for which he is receiving B-blocker. Physical examination is unremarkable. Rectal exam for the prostate showed a prostate of around 50 grams that is firm in consistency. Urine analysis is negative for nitrate, showed 2 pus cells/high power field and no microhematuria. Which of the following is the most likely Diagnosis?","opts":[{"k":"A","t":"Urethral stricture disease."},{"k":"B","t":"Cancer of prostate."},{"k":"C","t":"Cystitis."},{"k":"D","t":"Benign prostatic hyperplasia."},{"k":"E","t":"Urinary bladder stone."}],"ans":"D","ansAdded":false,"srcNote":"(LUTS, BPH, UTI)","img":[],"expl":"An older man with storage and voiding LUTS, a smooth firm enlarged (~50 g) prostate and clean urine = benign prostatic hyperplasia.","why":{"A":"Stricture gives a normal-sized prostate and a history of urethral injury/infection.","B":"Cancer feels hard and nodular.","C":"Cystitis needs infected urine (nitrites, many pus cells).","D":"Correct.","E":"Bladder stones cause haematuria and interrupted stream; the exam points to the prostate."}},{"id":"dd9f17d1","n":4,"exam":["final"],"year":439,"years":[439],"rep":1,"appears":"","stem":"8 months old come with pylonephritis for first time what is appropriate management","opts":[{"k":"A","t":"Prophylactic abx"},{"k":"B","t":"Open reflux surgery"},{"k":"C","t":"Ureteral injection"}],"ans":"A","ansAdded":false,"srcNote":"Abbreviated source recall; choice D is blank. No additional clinical details are supplied.","img":[],"expl":"A first febrile UTI in an infant (often from VUR) → treat, then prophylactic antibiotics while US/VCUG are done; surgery only for breakthrough infections or high-grade reflux.","why":{"A":"Correct.","B":"Open surgery isn't the first step.","C":"Endoscopic injection isn't the first step."}},{"id":"a8867e89","n":5,"exam":["final"],"year":439,"years":[439],"rep":1,"appears":"","stem":"BPH patient on tamselucin. Came after with Prostate size was 20g and PSA 0.2ng/dL with negative urinalysis what is the Best management?","opts":[{"k":"A","t":"TURP"},{"k":"B","t":"Open prostatectomy"},{"k":"C","t":"View urethrotomy"},{"k":"D","t":"Radiation therapy"}],"ans":"A","ansAdded":false,"srcNote":"(LUTS, UTI, BPH) Abbreviated source recall; the reason for proceeding to surgery is not stated.","img":[],"expl":"Persistent bothersome symptoms despite tamsulosin (failed medical therapy) in a small gland with a normal PSA → surgery: TURP (the standard operation for small to moderate glands).","why":{"A":"Correct.","B":"Open prostatectomy is for very large glands.","C":"Urethrotomy treats strictures.","D":"Radiation is for cancer."}},{"id":"6a04e5d5","n":6,"exam":["final"],"year":438,"years":[438,437,436,429],"rep":4,"appears":"Final: 438, 437, 436, 429","stem":"A 60 year old man, a known case of benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) but not on medications, presented to the outpatient clinic complaining of frequency of urination, nocturia and dribbling. He has noticed that his urine flow is worsening with time. He is really limited by his volding symptoms and wants to discuss his treatment options with you. Which ONE of the following is an absolute indication for surgical treatment of his condition?","opts":[{"k":"A","t":"Symptoms that can be managed with oral medications"},{"k":"B","t":"One episode of gross hematuria that is BPH related"},{"k":"C","t":"Bladder stone formation secondary to bladder outlet obstruction"},{"k":"D","t":"Prostate size of 100 grams with mild lower urinary tract symptoms"}],"ans":"C","ansAdded":false,"srcNote":"(LUTS, BPH, UTI) Source note (Final 436 F Q45): D. Bladder stone formation secondary to the obstruction D (Question was cancelled and marks were distributed) (LUTS, BPH, UTI)","img":[],"expl":"Bladder stones from outlet obstruction are an absolute indication for surgery (as are recurrent retention, recurrent UTIs, renal insufficiency and recurrent gross haematuria).","why":{"A":"Symptoms controlled on medication are not an indication.","B":"A single episode of haematuria isn't — recurrent gross haematuria is.","C":"Correct.","D":"Size alone isn't an indication."}},{"id":"b454fac9","n":7,"exam":["final"],"year":438,"years":[438],"rep":1,"appears":"","stem":"A 65 years old comes with reduced urine flow, no other urinary symptoms. Which of the following investigations is mandatory to be done?","opts":[{"k":"A","t":"PSA"},{"k":"B","t":"Urine analysis"},{"k":"C","t":"Pressure flow study"},{"k":"D","t":"Ultrasound"}],"ans":"B","ansAdded":false,"srcNote":"","img":[],"expl":"Urinalysis is mandatory in every man with LUTS — to exclude infection and haematuria (which changes the work-up).","why":{"A":"PSA is recommended if it would change management, not mandatory for everyone.","B":"Correct.","C":"Pressure-flow studies are for selected patients.","D":"US is optional."}},{"id":"e0451ff2","n":8,"exam":["final","mid"],"year":437,"years":[437,435,432,431,429,427],"rep":8,"appears":"Midterm: 437, 427 | Final: 435, 432, 431, 429, 427","stem":"A 72 years-old male presented to the emergency department with suprapubic pain and dribbling of urine. He was unable to pass urine for the last 8 hours. Examination revealed suprapupic tenderness that is dull on percussion. Which of the following is the appropriate initial step in his management?","opts":[{"k":"A","t":"Finasteride 5mg."},{"k":"B","t":"Foley's catheter insertion."},{"k":"C","t":"Intravenous antibiotics."},{"k":"D","t":"Intramuscular Morphine."}],"ans":"B","ansAdded":false,"srcNote":"(Luts, BPH, UTI) Source note (Midterm 437 B Q27): A 48-year-old male had inguinal hernia repair, following his surgery he failed to urinate on his own, following two in and out bladder catheterization he still couldn't urinate. What is the management for this patient’s urinary retention? D. Insert foley catheter with trial of void in few days D (Surgical Complications)","img":[],"expl":"Acute urinary retention (painful inability to void, dull tender suprapubic mass) → immediate bladder drainage with a urethral (Foley) catheter.","why":{"A":"Finasteride takes months to work.","B":"Correct.","C":"No sign of infection.","D":"Analgesia doesn't relieve the retention."}},{"id":"5a830338","n":9,"exam":["final"],"year":436,"years":[436,434,433],"rep":4,"appears":"Final: 436, 434, 433","stem":"A 60y gentleman presented with mild urinary symptoms. Rectal examination revealed a small prostate with nodules. PSA was normal. What’s your next step in management ?","opts":[{"k":"A","t":"Transrectal US"},{"k":"B","t":"Transrectal US with biopsy"},{"k":"C","t":"Abdominal MRI"},{"k":"D","t":"CT of pelvis"}],"ans":"B","ansAdded":false,"srcNote":"(LUTS, BPH, UTI)","img":[],"expl":"A nodular prostate on DRE suspicious of cancer must be biopsied even with a normal PSA (some cancers don't raise it) → transrectal US–guided biopsy (MRI-targeted biopsy where available).","why":{"A":"US alone can't diagnose cancer.","B":"Correct.","C":"An abdominal MRI isn't the next step (prostate MRI may guide biopsy).","D":"CT is for staging, not diagnosis."}},{"id":"1ff0950a","n":10,"exam":["final"],"year":436,"years":[436],"rep":1,"appears":"","stem":"What is the most common cause of catheter associated UTI?","opts":[{"k":"A","t":"Urethral meatal bacteria"},{"k":"B","t":"Bacterial antimicrobial resistance"},{"k":"C","t":"Improper catheterization technique"},{"k":"D","t":"Break in drainage system"}],"ans":"C","ansAdded":false,"srcNote":"","img":[],"expl":"Source key: improper catheterization technique. Most CAUTIs arise from organisms entering along the catheter — breaks in aseptic insertion and catheter care, and long duration.","why":{"A":"The route, but not the keyed answer.","B":"Resistance affects treatment, not causation.","C":"Correct (source key).","D":"Another contributor, less common."}},{"id":"84e1c89f","n":11,"exam":["final"],"year":436,"years":[436],"rep":1,"appears":"","stem":"What is the organization of micturition reflex?","opts":[{"k":"A","t":"Brain Cortex"},{"k":"B","t":"Brainstem"},{"k":"C","t":"Pelvic nerves"},{"k":"D","t":"Sacral spinal nerve"}],"ans":"B","ansAdded":false,"srcNote":"Not sure The question was canceled","img":[],"expl":"Micturition is a sacral spinal reflex (S2–S4) coordinated by the pontine micturition centre in the brainstem, under voluntary cortical control.","why":{"A":"The cortex controls it voluntarily but doesn't organize the reflex.","B":"Correct.","C":"Pelvic nerves carry the reflex; they don't coordinate it.","D":"The sacral centre is the reflex arc; coordination is pontine."}},{"id":"7a411bdc","n":12,"exam":["final"],"year":435,"years":[435,434,433,432,431],"rep":7,"appears":"Final: 435, 434, 433, 432, 431","stem":"A 60 year-old- man presented with weak urinary flow. Imaging is provided. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","opts":[{"k":"A","t":"Urethral stone."},{"k":"B","t":"Urethral trauma."},{"k":"C","t":"Urethral stricture."},{"k":"D","t":"Benign static hyperplasia."}],"ans":"C","ansAdded":false,"srcNote":"(LUTS, UTI,BPH) Source note (Final 435 A Q41 (p. 138)): A 29 year old man is complaining of poor urine stream, post void dribbling and frequency. What is the most likely diagnosis? C. Urethral stricture. C (LUTS, UTI, BPH)","img":["data:image/jpeg;base64,"],"expl":"The retrograde urethrogram shows a narrowed segment of the urethra with dilatation behind it = urethral stricture.","why":{"A":"A stone would be a filling defect.","B":"Trauma shows contrast extravasation.","C":"Correct.","D":"BPH narrows the prostatic urethra and isn't diagnosed on a urethrogram."}},{"id":"3ffc9e0d","n":13,"exam":["final"],"year":434,"years":[434,433],"rep":2,"appears":"Final: 434, 433","stem":"A 60-year-old man known to have diabetes mellitus and hypertension was diagnosed with chronic retention and was having mildly elevated creatinine. A Foley catheter was inserted which drained 800cc clear urine. Which of the following should be monitored on admission?","opts":[{"k":"A","t":"Renal impairment."},{"k":"B","t":"Possibility of hematuria."},{"k":"C","t":"Post obstructive diuresis."},{"k":"D","t":"Trial of removing the catheter."}],"ans":"C","ansAdded":false,"srcNote":"(no lecture about it)","img":[],"expl":"After decompressing chronic retention with renal impairment, the kidneys can pour out salt and water (post-obstructive diuresis) → monitor urine output, fluids and electrolytes.","why":{"A":"Creatinine is followed, but the immediate danger is diuresis.","B":"Haematuria from decompression is usually minor.","C":"Correct.","D":"Trial without catheter comes much later."}},{"id":"e52d129d","n":14,"exam":["final"],"year":429,"years":[429],"rep":1,"appears":"","stem":"Which of the following is the initial management of 65 years old male BPH who is symptomatic and bothered with (60 grams) prostate?","opts":[{"k":"A","t":"Beta-blockers only"},{"k":"B","t":"Combination of a-Blockers and 5 a reductase inhibitors"},{"k":"C","t":"Transurethral resection of the prostate"},{"k":"D","t":"Foley's catheterization"}],"ans":"B","ansAdded":false,"srcNote":"(LUTS, BPH, UTI)","img":[],"expl":"A bothersome, symptomatic man with a large (60 g) prostate → combination of an α1-blocker (fast relief) and a 5ARI (shrinks the gland, lowers progression).","why":{"A":"β-blockers don't treat BPH.","B":"Correct.","C":"Surgery needs an indication or failed medical therapy.","D":"Catheterization is for retention."}},{"id":"d7423380","n":15,"exam":["final","mid"],"year":428,"years":[428,427],"rep":3,"appears":"Midterm: 427 | Final: 428, 427","stem":"All of the followings will present with voiding LUTS except :","opts":[{"k":"A","t":"Diabetic neuropathy"},{"k":"B","t":"BPH"},{"k":"C","t":"Urethral stricture"},{"k":"D","t":"Cystitis"},{"k":"E","t":"myelomeningocele"}],"ans":"D","ansAdded":false,"srcNote":"(LUTS, BPH, UTI)","img":[],"expl":"Cystitis causes storage (irritative) symptoms — frequency, urgency, dysuria — not voiding symptoms.","why":{"A":"Diabetic neuropathy causes a weak detrusor → voiding symptoms.","B":"BPH causes voiding symptoms.","C":"Stricture causes voiding symptoms.","D":"Correct.","E":"Myelomeningocele (neurogenic bladder) causes voiding symptoms."}},{"id":"b4e93c2a","n":16,"exam":["final","mid"],"year":427,"years":[427],"rep":2,"appears":"Midterm: 427 | Final: 427","stem":"In a patient with BPH, all are true except:","opts":[{"k":"A","t":"Cystogram CT is ONLY done in patient with hematuria"},{"k":"B","t":"The size of the prostate in digital examination could not expect the actual obstructive symptom that the patient has."},{"k":"C","t":"Cystoscopy is done for patients with obstructive symptoms caused by BPH"}],"ans":"C","ansAdded":false,"srcNote":", not sure. (LUTS, BPH, UTI) The source supplies three choices and marks its answer as uncertain.","img":[],"expl":"Cystoscopy isn't routine for obstructive symptoms from BPH — it is for haematuria or before surgery. DRE size doesn't predict symptoms (true).","why":{"A":"(Garbled recall) — imaging of the bladder is reserved for haematuria; treated as true.","B":"True — size doesn't predict obstruction.","C":"Correct (the false statement)."}},{"id":"1d86ce39","n":17,"exam":["mid"],"year":427,"years":[427],"rep":1,"appears":"","stem":"voiding lower urinary tract symptoms can present in all except:","opts":[{"k":"A","t":"Cystitis"},{"k":"B","t":"BPH"},{"k":"C","t":"Spina bifida"},{"k":"D","t":"Diabetic cystopathy"}],"ans":"A","ansAdded":false,"srcNote":"","img":[],"expl":"Cystitis gives storage symptoms; BPH, spina bifida and diabetic cystopathy all cause voiding symptoms.","why":{"A":"Correct.","B":"Causes voiding LUTS.","C":"Causes voiding LUTS.","D":"Causes voiding LUTS."}},{"id":"d805385a","n":18,"exam":["mid"],"year":426,"years":[426],"rep":1,"appears":"","stem":"What is the cause of urge incontinence?","opts":[{"k":"A","t":"Detrusor instability is the cause"}],"ans":null,"ansAdded":false,"srcNote":"incomplete Q The source supplies one statement without an answer key.","img":[],"ansText":"Detrusor overactivity (instability) — involuntary bladder contractions cause a sudden urge and leakage before reaching the toilet."},{"id":"23ce4d63","n":19,"exam":["mid"],"year":426,"years":[426],"rep":1,"appears":"","stem":"LUTS","opts":[{"k":"A","t":"urge incontinence is drop of urine before reaching toilet"},{"k":"B","t":"Can happen in young female"},{"k":"C","t":"Hesitancy is straining b4 urination"}],"ans":"B","ansAdded":false,"srcNote":"“incomplete Q” Incomplete source recall; no missing wording or options have been inferred.","img":[],"expl":"LUTS are not limited to older men — young women get them (cystitis, OAB). Hesitancy is a delay in starting the stream, not straining; urge incontinence is leakage with a sudden urge (the recall's wording is loose).","why":{"A":"Loosely describes it but the defining feature is leakage associated with urgency.","B":"Correct.","C":"Hesitancy = delay in starting; straining is a separate symptom."}},{"id":"ef448925","n":20,"exam":["mid"],"year":426,"years":[426],"rep":1,"appears":"","stem":"urethral Stricture","opts":[{"k":"A","t":"Ascending urethrogram is the investigation of choice"},{"k":"B","t":"Treatment by dilation"},{"k":"C","t":"happen in ALL young male with urine drop"}],"ans":"A","ansAdded":false,"srcNote":", incomplete Q Incomplete source recall; no missing wording or options have been inferred.","img":[],"expl":"The retrograde (ascending) urethrogram is the investigation of choice — it shows the site and length of the stricture.","why":{"A":"Correct.","B":"Dilatation is only one option (urethrotomy, urethroplasty).","C":"Strictures don't affect \"all young males\"."}},{"id":"94ce7578","n":21,"exam":["mid"],"year":426,"years":[426],"rep":1,"appears":"","stem":"all can be used for LUTS except","opts":[{"k":"A","t":"Vasopressin"},{"k":"B","t":"Finasteride"},{"k":"C","t":"Alfuzosin"},{"k":"D","t":"Tamsulosin"}],"ans":"A","ansAdded":false,"srcNote":"","img":[],"expl":"Finasteride (5ARI) and alfuzosin/tamsulosin (α1-blockers) treat LUTS from BPH; vasopressin isn't used (only its analogue desmopressin, for nocturnal polyuria).","why":{"A":"Correct.","B":"Used for BPH.","C":"Used for BPH.","D":"Used for BPH."}},{"id":"61683dba","n":22,"exam":["mid"],"year":426,"years":[426],"rep":1,"appears":"","stem":"To differentiate between DM and BPH in a pt. have both and LUTS","opts":[{"k":"A","t":"US"},{"k":"B","t":"Cystoscopy"},{"k":"C","t":"Void diary Urodynamics flow and flow"}],"ans":"C","ansAdded":false,"srcNote":", incomplete Q Incomplete source recall; no missing wording or options have been inferred.","img":[],"expl":"Urodynamics (pressure-flow studies) distinguish outlet obstruction (BPH: high pressure, low flow) from a weak, underactive diabetic bladder (low pressure, low flow).","why":{"A":"US shows size, not function.","B":"Cystoscopy shows anatomy, not function.","C":"Correct."}},{"id":"a4825903","n":23,"exam":["mid"],"year":426,"years":[426],"rep":1,"appears":"","stem":"BPH all true except","opts":[{"k":"A","t":"Indication for surgery is recurrent UTI"},{"k":"B","t":"immediate TURB"},{"k":"C","t":"Can be treated medically with 5-alpha reductase inhibitors"},{"k":"D","t":"Effect central zone"}],"ans":"B","ansAdded":false,"srcNote":"","img":[],"expl":"BPH is treated stepwise (watchful waiting, medication) — surgery needs an indication, so \"immediate TURP\" is false.","why":{"A":"True.","B":"Correct (the false statement).","C":"True.","D":"BPH arises in the transition (central periurethral) zone — older texts call it central."}}]